                <pb facs="0419" n="407" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg407"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War, 1939/35: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Hungary</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Travers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Budapest</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-01T23:00:00+01:00">September 1,
                            1939—11 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 1—7:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>173. Conditions in Hungary calm and no great military movements visible. The
                    Chief of the Political Section of the Foreign Office reiterated tonight to me
                    that Hungary’s hands are still free and that Hungary will never fight Poland. He
                    envisages Hungary maintaining neutrality for some time to come and said it is
                    probable that a strict censorship will soon be imposed in order to assist in
                    maintaining that neutrality.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Travers</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
